<%# console.credits — credits.php. The legacy screen is a rendering of an API response:
    wp_credits() (wp-admin/includes/credits.php) fetches
    api.wordpress.org/core/credits/1.1/ over the network and prints the groups it returns —
    project leaders, core contributors, props, translation validators, external libraries.

    Two things make that untranslatable here rather than merely rebrandable:
      · this build makes no external requests, so there is no response to render; and
      · DEV-009 drops project identity — the people named in that response contributed to
        WordPress, not to this rebuild, and printing them here would claim otherwise.
    So the IA is kept (title, tabs, a lede block, then sections) and refilled with what can
    actually be stated: where this code came from, and which libraries it runs on. NO
    contributor, sponsor or team is named, because this build has no roster to name. -%>
<% @page_title = "Credits" %>
<h1 class="wp-heading-inline">Credits</h1>
<hr class="wp-header-end">

<%= render "console/info/nav" %>

<div class="about-section">
  <p class="about-lede">This build has no contributor roster. The legacy screen filled this
  page from a directory hosted by the project it belonged to; this build makes no external
  requests, and inventing names to fill the space would be a lie. What can be stated is
  where the code came from.</p>
</div>

<div class="about-section">
  <h2>Provenance</h2>
  <p>The behaviour reproduced here was reverse-engineered from a WordPress 7.2-alpha source
  corpus under the Reversa framework, screen by screen and rule by rule. The legacy
  software is the work of the WordPress project and its contributors. This build reproduces
  its observable behaviour, not its authorship, and carries none of its project
  identity.</p>
  <p>The specifications, business rules, data model and deviation log the rebuild was
  written against live in the repository under <code>_reversa_sdd/</code>. Every departure
  from legacy behaviour is recorded there with the reason it was approved, which is the
  nearest thing this build has to a changelog with names on it.</p>
</div>

<%# credits.php's `External Libraries` group heading — LITERAL (credits.php:135, one of the
    strings the file keeps translatable for the API response). The rows are not an API
    response: they are the gems actually loaded into this process, read at render time. -%>
<div class="about-section">
  <h2>External Libraries</h2>
  <% if @libraries.any? %>
    <table class="about-facts">
      <tbody>
        <% @libraries.each do |name, version| %>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><%= name %></th>
            <td><%= version %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="description">The direct runtime dependencies this application declares, with
    the version actually loaded when this page was rendered. The block editor island
    additionally bundles React and React DOM; see <code>package.json</code>.</p>
  <% else %>
    <p class="no-items">No runtime dependency information is available in this
    environment.</p>
  <% end %>
</div>

<%# about.php:406-412 — the return link, LITERAL. -%>
<div class="about-return">
  <a href="/console">Go to Dashboard &rarr; Home</a>
</div>
